# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r2 has "holds_theory → not_hostile"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.3 have "holds_theory → not_hostile" and "¬commend_generosity"; they differ when only "people maintain the theory that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm", "the theory cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths" and "we can commend the generosity of the theory's dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for new truths" are true, which the first allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "people maintain the theory that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm", "the theory cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths" and "we can commend the generosity of the theory's dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for new truths" are true. Is S1, "A theory which maintains that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm, cannot be charged with being intentionally hostile to the reception of new truths; but we cannot commend the generosity of its dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for them.", true then?
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r2 has "holds_theory" and "confined_enough"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.3 have "confined_enough"; they differ when only "this view is mostly confined to persons who think new truths may have been desirable once but that we have had enough of them now" is true, which the second allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "this view is mostly confined to persons who think new truths may have been desirable once but that we have had enough of them now" is true and "people maintain the theory that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm" is false. Is S5, "People who defend this mode of treating benefactors, cannot be supposed to set much value on the benefit; and I believe this view of the subject is mostly confined to the sort of persons who think that new truths may have been desirable once, but that we have had enough of them now.", true then?
mill-liberty-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r2 has "martyrdom_justified ∧ great_service → ¬commend_generosity" and "martyrdom_justified ∧ great_service → ¬value_benefit"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.3 have "great_service ∧ martyrdom_justified → ¬value_benefit"; they differ when only "we can commend the generosity of the theory's dealing with the persons to whom mankind are indebted for new truths", "to discover to the world something which deeply concerns it, or to prove it mistaken on some vital point, is as important a service as a human being can render to his fellow-creatures" and "that the authors of such splendid benefits should be requited by martyrdom is not a deplorable error but the normal and justifiable state of things" are true, which the second allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: mill-liberty-1.logic.r2 has "¬value_benefit"; mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-liberty-1.logic.r2.3 have "holds_theory → ¬value_benefit"; they differ when only "people who defend this mode of treating benefactors set much value on the benefit" is true, which the second allows
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps mill-liberty-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "people who defend this mode of treating benefactors set much value on the benefit" is true and "people maintain the theory that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm" is false. Is the conclusion, "People who defend this mode of treating benefactors, cannot be supposed to set much value on the benefit; and I believe this view of the subject is mostly confined to the sort of persons who think that new truths may have been desirable once, but that we have had enough of them now.", true then?
mill-liberty-1.md:1: argue/negation: mill-liberty-1.logic.r2 P5 (S4): the sentence says "instantly", but P5 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if people maintain the theory that truth may justifiably be persecuted because persecution cannot possibly do it any harm, then the propounder of a new truth should stand, like the Locrian proposer of a new law, with a halter round his neck, to be tightened if the assembly did not adopt his proposition"
